To print a stack trace in Linux without allocating memory on the heap, you can use the backtrace() and backtrace_symbols_fd() functions provided by GNU libc. These functions allow you to get the stack trace and print it directly to a file descriptor without allocating memory on the heap.

You can do this in C with the following code:

```C
#include <execinfo.h>
#include <unistd.h>

void print_stack_trace() {
  const size_t max_depth = 100;
  size_t stack_depth;
  void *stack_addrs[max_depth];
  char **stack_strings;

  stack_depth = backtrace(stack_addrs, max_depth);
  backtrace_symbols_fd(stack_addrs, stack_depth, STDERR_FILENO);
}
```

In this code, backtrace() gets the stack addresses and backtrace_symbols_fd() writes the symbols corresponding to these addresses to STDERR.

This method doesn't allocate memory on the heap because backtrace_symbols_fd() writes directly to a file descriptor. It's important to note that this method might not get all the information you would get with a full-fledged stack trace that uses heap memory.

Also, this method might not work with all compilers or on all systems. It's a GNU-specific feature. You might have to compile with the `-rdynamic` flag to get the function names. 

This method can be useful in situations where you have a memory corruption issue and you want to avoid further damage by not allocating more memory. But in general, if you're not in such a situation, it would be better to use a full-fledged debugging tool like gdb.

The Stoer-Wagner algorithm is used to find the minimum cut of an undirected graph. It is an efficient algorithm that works in polynomial time. Unfortunately, as of my knowledge cutoff in 2023, there is no native Swift library that implements graph algorithms such as the Stoer-Wagner algorithm directly. However, I can provide you with a sample Swift implementation.

Please note that this implementation is for educational purposes and might not be the most efficient. The algorithm involves several steps including finding the most tightly connected vertex, merging vertices, and calculating the minimum cut. Here's a basic outline of the algorithm in Swift:

```swift
import Foundation

class Edge {
    let to: Int
    var weight: Int
    init(to: Int, weight: Int) {
        self.to = to
        self.weight = weight
    }
}

class Graph {
    var adjacencyList: [[Edge]]

    init(vertexCount: Int) {
        self.adjacencyList = Array(repeating: [], count: vertexCount)
    }

    func addEdge(from u: Int, to v: Int, weight: Int) {
        adjacencyList[u].append(Edge(to: v, weight: weight))
        adjacencyList[v].append(Edge(to: u, weight: weight))
    }

    func minimumCut() -> Int {
        var minCut = Int.max
        var vertices = Array(0..<adjacencyList.count)
        var weights = Array(repeating: 0, count: adjacencyList.count)
        var exist = Array(repeating: true, count: adjacencyList.count)
        var last = 0

        for _ in 0..<adjacencyList.count-1 {
            weights = Array(repeating: 0, count: adjacencyList.count)
            var used = Array(repeating: false, count: adjacencyList.count)
            var prev = 0

            for _ in 1..<vertices.count {
                var maxWeight = -1
                var maxVertex = -1

                for j in vertices where exist[j] && !used[j] {
                    if weights[j] > maxWeight {
                        maxWeight = weights[j]
                        maxVertex = j
                    }
                }

                if maxVertex == -1 {
                    return minCut
                }

                used[maxVertex] = true
                last = maxVertex
                prev = maxVertex

                for edge in adjacencyList[maxVertex] {
                    if exist[edge.to] {
                        weights[edge.to] += edge.weight
                    }
                }
            }

            minCut = min(minCut, weights[last])

            // Merge vertices
            exist[prev] = false
            for i in 0..<adjacencyList[prev].count {
                let edge = adjacencyList[prev][i]
                if exist[edge.to] {
                    adjacencyList[last].append(Edge(to: edge.to, weight: edge.weight))
                    adjacencyList[edge.to].append(Edge(to: last, weight: edge.weight))
                }
            }

            // Remove self loops
            adjacencyList[last].removeAll { edge in
                !exist[edge.to] || edge.to == last
            }
        }
        return minCut
    }
}

// Usage example
let graph = Graph(vertexCount: 4)
graph.addEdge(from: 0, to: 1, weight: 1)
graph.addEdge(from: 1, to: 2, weight: 1)
graph.addEdge(from: 2, to: 3, weight: 1)
graph.addEdge(from: 3, to: 0, weight: 1)
graph.addEdge(from: 0, to: 2, weight: 1)

let result = graph.minimumCut()
print(result)  // Should output the min-cut value of the graph
```

This code defines a `Graph` class, which you can use to construct an undirected graph and then calculate the minimum cut using the `minimumCut` method. The `minimumCut` method is an implementation of the Stoer-Wagner algorithm.

Keep in mind that this code is a simple implementation and lacks optimizations like priority queues, which could improve the performance of the algorithm. For large graphs or performance-critical applications, you might want to use a more optimized version or a different programming language that has libraries specifically optimized for graph algorithms.

Here are popup.html and styles.css to go along with the popup.js code:

popup.html:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
  <script src="popup.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
  <button id="openPopup">Open Popup</button>

  <div class="popup" id="popup">
    <div class="popup-content">
      <span class="close" id="closePopup">&times;</span>  
      <p>Hello! I'm a popup.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
```

styles.css:
```css
.popup {
  display: none;
  position: fixed;
  top: 0; left: 0;
  width: 100%; height: 100%;
}

.popup-content {
  position: absolute;
  top: 50%; left: 50%;
  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
  background: #fff;
  padding: 1rem;
}

.close {
  position: absolute;
  top: 0; right: 0;
  padding: 1rem;
  cursor: pointer;
}
```

This provides the HTML with a button to open the popup, the popup div, and the CSS to position and style the popup. The popup.js code can then be used to show and hide the popup when the button is clicked.

