In CSS and HTML you can display attribute values in different ways, depending on the context and purpose. Here are a few examples:

1. Using the `attr()` function in CSS:
```css
div::before {
  content: "The title attribute value is: " attr(title);
}
```
This will display the `title` attribute value of the `div` element as a string.

2. Using JavaScript to access and display attribute values:
```html
<div id="example" title="Example attribute value">
  <p>Click the button to display the title attribute value.</p>
  <button onclick="displayAttribute()">Display attribute value</button>
</div>

<script>
function displayAttribute() {
  const exampleDiv = document.getElementById('example');
  alert('The title attribute value is: ' + exampleDiv.getAttribute('title'));
}
</script>
```
This example will display the `title` attribute value of the `div` element with the ID `example` in an alert box when the button is clicked.

3. Using the `data-*` attribute in HTML and CSS:
```html
<div class="example" data-attribute="Example attribute value">
  <p>The data attribute value is: <span class="attribute-value"></span></p>
</div>

<script>
document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
  const exampleDiv = document.querySelector('.example');
  const attributeValue = exampleDiv.getAttribute('data-attribute');
  const attributeValueElement = document.querySelector('.attribute-value');
  attributeValueElement.textContent = attributeValue;
});
</script>
```
This example will display the `data-attribute` value of the `div` element with the class `example` in the `span` element with the class `attribute-value` when the page is loaded.
